    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Late-onset Alzheimer’s disease (AD), in comparison to familial AD, accounts for 95% of all AD cases. Previous genomic association studies have shown the e4 allele of the apolipoprotein E gene (ApoE4) to significantly increase the risk of late-onset AD. Using single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) and structural magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) data of 1018 subjects from the Alzheimer’s Disease Neuroimaging Initiative, this study investigated how SNPs in 50 genes functionally related to, or co-expressed with ApoE modulate the risk associated with ApoE4 and whether any genetic variant within these 50 genes shows sex-specific interaction. Three linear models were constructed to separately quantify the effect of 1) SNP only 2) SNP-by-ApoE4 interaction 3) SNP-by-Sex interaction on hippocampal gray matter density extracted from structural MRIs. After filtering for SNPs with a minor allele frequency over 0.05, a total of 7760 SNPs were tested. No SNP remained significant after adjusting for multiple comparisons (adjusted p-value threshold = 5.04 x 10-5). A SNP in the aminolevulinate dehydratase gene, rs818691, came close to the threshold in the SNP-by-Sex model with a p-value of 5.26 x 10-5. Our results suggest that no common polymorphisms in genes known to be functionally related to, or co-expressed with ApoE, contributes significantly to hippocampal atrophy and that ApoE modulates risk of AD through pathways that have yet to be clarified.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
